  "annotation": {
    "schema_version": 5,
    "type": "bugfix",
    "problem": [
      {
        "text": "Linear previously retained the exact output width, while EXL3 quantization requires its transposed weight's output dimension to be divisible by 128. Nonaligned vocabulary projections could violate that requirement.",
        "basis": "source",
        "evidence": [
          "call_c7e5c69d9e0646549110b604e5d7e93c",
          "call_149990c644184411bb771111ed351bf7",
          "call_c4bce6f26d4746a585267aa4b95af671",
          "call_525c45ad2b8d47b397d97ead5bc86301"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "reasoning": [
      {
        "text": "The patch rounds output widths upward and zero-pads loaded weights and optional biases before constructing LinearFP16, so conversion receives physically padded tensors rather than only enlarged dimension metadata.",
        "basis": "source",
        "evidence": [
          "call_4e3764fc6d944d5ba1aa2adee5053803",
          "call_c7e5c69d9e0646549110b604e5d7e93c"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "impact": [
      {
        "text": "Padding applies to every Linear by default, not only vocabulary heads. Already aligned dimensions are unchanged; unaligned dimensions consume additional storage and produce wider outputs because Linear.forward does not trim them.",
        "basis": "source",
        "evidence": [
          "call_4e3764fc6d944d5ba1aa2adee5053803",
          "call_c7e5c69d9e0646549110b604e5d7e93c",
          "call_149990c644184411bb771111ed351bf7"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "source_notes": [
      {
        "text": "An earlier annotation reported a lowercase/uppercase EXL3 metadata discriminator mismatch.",
        "basis": "ancestor",
        "evidence": [
          "call_c1b07069924d457bafe093a3268eabf4"
        ]
      },
      {
        "text": "That mismatch is already removed in the inspected parent: both Linear.quant_format_id and its metadata consumer use lowercase 'exl3'. This patch leaves those paths unchanged.",
        "basis": "source",
        "evidence": [
          "call_c7e5c69d9e0646549110b604e5d7e93c",
          "call_4e99eae3dcd247a8b8684ae52bf23843",
          "call_4e3764fc6d944d5ba1aa2adee5053803"
        ]
      }
    ],
    "limitations": [
      "The arithmetic check executes a scalar transcription, not repository tensor operations, conversion, or GPU kernels.",
      "End-to-end compatibility of padded outputs with generation and non-vocabulary projections was not established.",
      "The fused-weight branch pads bias before slicing it; compatibility across fused bias layouts was not executed or established."
    ],
    "links": [
      "c8b4bd5454414b046a38344f0aa41b6e1d80d54d"
    ],
    "chapters": [
      {
        "id": "modules",
        "title": "Align linear output dimensions through zero padding",
        "claims": [
          {
            "text": "Linear.__init__ adds optional pad_to=128, preserves the requested width as out_features_unpadded, and computes ceil(out_features / pad_to) \u00d7 pad_to. Here 'odd' means nonaligned, not merely an odd integer. The default matches the quantizer's size_n % 128 assertion.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_4e3764fc6d944d5ba1aa2adee5053803",
              "call_c4bce6f26d4746a585267aa4b95af671"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "pad_out returns None or already-aligned tensors unchanged. Otherwise it allocates zeros with the original dtype and device, copies weight rows or bias entries into the prefix, and returns contiguous storage. For fused weights, load_fp16 selects the requested row range before padding the weight.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_4e3764fc6d944d5ba1aa2adee5053803"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "LinearFP16 transposes the padded weight into input-by-output layout. convert_exl3 retrieves that tensor for quantization, and weights_numel now counts the padded width. This aligns storage, quantization input and reported dimensions, at the cost of retaining extra output channels rather than trimming them at the Linear boundary.",
            "basis": "source",
            "evidence": [
              "call_149990c644184411bb771111ed351bf7",
              "call_c7e5c69d9e0646549110b604e5d7e93c",
              "call_4e3764fc6d944d5ba1aa2adee5053803"
            ]
          },
          {
            "text": "A standard-library transcription verified rounding invariants for widths 1\u20139999: the result is divisible by 128 and adds between 0 and 127 channels. Example widths 129 and 151665 round to 256 and 151680 respectively.",
            "basis": "execution",
            "evidence": [
              "call_93c261f122ea48bd9ce7e2db6dd40875"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "findings": [],
    "summary": "Pads Linear output widths and loaded FP16 tensors to multiples of 128, accommodating nonaligned vocabulary projections before EXL3 conversion."
  },
